            {
              "id": "glog-a1546978-4a7b-488b-a6ad-cab6d3e84001",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a fixed value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random number sequences. This vulnerability can be exploited by attackers to predict the outcomes of supposedly random operations, which can compromise security, especially in cryptographic applications or when generating unique identifiers.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a more secure source of randomness for seeding. The C++11 standard library provides `std::random_device`, which is a non-deterministic random number generator that can be used to seed other random number generators.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `srand(0)` with a more secure seeding method using `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <random> // Required for std::random_device and std::mt19937\n\nint main() {\n    // Create a random device and use it to seed the random number generator\n    std::random_device rd;\n    std::mt19937 gen(rd());\n\n    // Generate a random number\n    std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(1, 100);\n    int random_number = dis(gen);\n\n    std::cout << \"Random number: \" << random_number << std::endl;\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard library headers are required:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n- `<random>`: For random number generation utilities.\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
